const e="/history",i="download",o="The History of LimeWire: 2000 to Today",n="How LimeWire started in 2000, why the record labels shut the file-sharing client down in 2010, what happened to the download, and what LimeWire is today.",a="2026-10-13",t=[{question:"What was LimeWire?",answer:"LimeWire was a free peer-to-peer file-sharing client, written in Java and first released in 2000. It ran on Windows, macOS, Linux and Solaris, and connected to the Gnutella network, which had no central catalogue and no company server holding the index."},{question:"Who created LimeWire?",answer:"LimeWire was created by Mark Gorton, whose company Lime Wire LLC was founded in New York in 2000. The Gnutella protocol it used was written separately, by Justin Frankel and Tom Pepper at Nullsoft, earlier the same year."},{question:"Why was LimeWire shut down?",answer:"Thirteen record companies led by Arista Records sued Lime Wire LLC in 2006. On 11 May 2010 Judge Kimba Wood found the company liable for inducing copyright infringement, and on 26 October 2010 a permanent injunction required the file-sharing features to be switched off. Lime Wire LLC settled with the labels for $105 million in May 2011."},{question:"Where can I download the old LimeWire?",answer:"Nowhere, and no copy found elsewhere is safe to trust. The original client was disabled by court order in 2010 and is no longer distributed. limewire.com today is a browser-based file sharing and AI platform; there is nothing to install."},{question:"Is LimeWire the same company it was in the 2000s?",answer:"No. Lime Wire LLC, the company sued in 2006, no longer operates the brand. The LimeWire name and trademarks were acquired in 2022 by a different company, which rebuilt the product from scratch as a web service. Nothing of the original peer-to-peer network was carried over."},{question:"When did LimeWire come out?",answer:"The first version was released in 2000, by Lime Wire LLC in New York. LimeWire Basic followed in 2001 under the GNU General Public License, with a paid LimeWire Pro edition alongside it. By the time the record labels sued, the client had close to four million users a day. Distribution of the file-sharing features ended with the court injunction of 26 October 2010."},{question:"Can I still download music with LimeWire?",answer:"No, and today’s LimeWire is not that kind of service. The old client searched the Gnutella network, where other people’s computers offered the files; that network connection was switched off in 2010 and nothing on limewire.com replaces it. There is no catalogue here to search for songs. What you can do is upload music you already own — your own recordings, your own purchased files, your own backups — and send it to someone as a link."},{question:"Can I download movies or films with LimeWire?",answer:"No. There is no film library, no search over other people’s computers, and no way to find copyrighted films here — sharing those without permission is exactly what the 2010 court case was about. LimeWire today moves files you already have: you upload a video, you get a link, you decide who gets it and you can revoke it later."}];export{n as H,i as a,t as b,e as c,a as d,o as e};
